Fuzz targets for the shim's one real parser: the settings dictionary a client
sends to `AddConnection`.

The arrangement is 0277's and the drivers are the daemon's, reached at
`../../../c/fuzz/` rather than copied. Three targets: two over the dictionary,
because they find different things and each is blind where the other is not,
and one over the names and paths a client chooses.

  dictionary   bytes off the bus, through `dbus_message_demarshal`, into the
               renderer. The real boundary, so the corpus is real marshalled
               calls. A one-byte flip inside a key name is cheap here, which
               is how the configuration injection of 0278 is reachable.
  settings     the dictionary built FROM the fuzzer's bytes: which group,
               which key, which type, how long. A 400-byte value is one byte
               of input, which is how a copy into a fixed buffer is reachable
               -- the wire target could not manage it in 2.7 million runs.

  names        the tail of every object path a client can hold, and the id a
               profile arrives with. Two of its answers leave the program --
               a UUID libnm parses, and a FILENAME under /etc/netcfgd/conf.d
               -- so it asserts the shape of both rather than only looking for
               a crash. Its state points at a socket that cannot exist, so a
               resolver's refresh fails fast instead of reaching a running
               daemon thousands of times a second.

What is NOT fuzzed here, and why, since a list of targets reads as a claim of
coverage: the dispatcher itself needs a `DBusConnection` and libdbus has no
in-process fake, so what a client's whole MESSAGE reaches is covered by
`tests/bus_test.c` against the tables and by `tests/live/nm.sh` against real
libnm. `AddConnection2`'s options dictionary is read inside a static handler
reachable only through dispatch. And what netcfgd answers arrives through the
client library: its JSON layer is fuzzed from the daemon's side
(`c/fuzz/document_json`), and the typed readers above it would need a socket.

`make_seeds.c` built the corpus; its output is committed so a run needs no
build step, and it is where to add the next shape. `shape.c` holds the
invariant that matters more than any crash: whatever the renderer wrote must
still be ONE network block.

    make -C adapter/netcfgd-nm/c fuzz-replay   the corpus, deterministic, in
                                               `make nm-c-test`
    make fuzz                                  these two and the daemon's five
